Session 18 summary — **crash_consistency is FIXED and PASSING**, plus two major test-infra defects found and fixed:

**v0.5.0 foreign-slice replay completed and verified (build `CB1C5FEF`).** Finished sess17's design: orchestrator `mxfs_xlog_recover_foreign_slice` in xfs_log.c (shadow xlog over the dead slice, private dummy AIL, slice = HB slot **% node_count** — the raw-slot version silently no-op'd on the first deploy), `xlog_dealloc_log` guard so the shadow can't NULL the live `mp->m_log`, lowest-live-slot election in disklock, dead-node notify callback through v5_mount, and a work-queue handler in xfs_mxfs_dlm.c with flush-before/after and unmount-time `cancel_work_sync`.

**Root cause of the remaining FAILs was the test knob, not the replay:** the `mxfs_lease_timeout_ms`/`mxfs_lease_duration_ms` params the criterion has always passed **never existed in the tree** — insmod silently ignores them, so death detection was hardcoded at 31×2s = 62s while the script waits 60s. Added a real `lease_timeout_ms` module param plumbed to the disklock dead threshold, plus an insmod-time param echo. Also fixed lib.sh to rmmod before insmod (an already-loaded module silently swallows INSMOD_OPTS). Result: detection at kill+16s, replay in 160ms, **crash_consistency PASS ×3** (2-node 123/123, 4-node 110/110 and 106/107), and **fence_during_write PASS** (lost=0).

**scaling_curve timeouts root-caused as infra:** my `make clean` had wiped `tools/mkfs_mxfs` and `fresh_cluster_mount` never checked `MKFS_OK` — mkfs failed silently and every "fresh" cluster reused the previous filesystem, turning stage 2 into a cross-node unlink storm (rm wedged 275s in `xlog_wait_on_iclog`). Tools rebuilt, lib.sh now hard-fails without MKFS_OK.

**Not done — criteria marker not written.** Remaining: clean reset, re-run scaling_curve (stage 1 measured healthy at 10.1s) and posix_semantics --nodes 16, watch for the test1 log-wedge recurring on a clean run, then the single end-to-end `verify_ship.sh` green run that the gate requires. Both ccmemory entries (`sess18-*`) carry the full state for the next session.

